        content: "Pathogens are disease-causing microorganisms. Bacterial diseases: Tuberculosis (TB) — caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, spread by airborne droplets, affects the lungs. MRSA — methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, a hospital-acquired infection resistant to many antibiotics. Salmonella — causes food poisoning from contaminated food (especially raw poultry and eggs). Cholera — caused by Vibrio cholerae, spread by contaminated water, causes severe diarrhoea. Viral diseases: Influenza — caused by the influenza virus, spread by droplets. HIV — Human Immunodeficiency Virus, attacks T-helper cells (CD4+), leading to AIDS (Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome). COVID-19 — caused by SARS-CoV-2, a coronavirus. Measles — highly contagious, preventable by MMR vaccine. Fungal diseases: Athlete's foot (Tinea pedis) — affects skin between toes. Ringworm — despite the name, caused by fungi, not worms. Protist diseases: Malaria — caused by Plasmodium, transmitted by female Anopheles mosquito, infects red blood cells and liver cells. Koch's postulates are the criteria used to establish that a specific microorganism causes a specific disease: (1) the organism must be found in all cases of the disease, (2) it must be isolated and grown in pure culture, (3) the cultured organism must cause the disease when introduced into a healthy host, (4) the organism must be re-isolated from the newly diseased host. Disease transmission routes include: droplet (airborne), direct contact, vectors (e.g. mosquitoes), contaminated food/water, and body fluids.",
