        title: "Evidence for Evolution",
        content: "Evolution is the change in the inherited characteristics of a population over successive generations. Evidence for evolution comes from multiple sources. Fossil record: Fossils are preserved remains or traces of organisms. They show a progression from simple to complex life forms over geological time. Transitional fossils (e.g. Archaeopteryx — features of both reptiles and birds) show links between groups. Gaps in the fossil record exist because fossilisation is rare and conditions must be specific (rapid burial, hard parts, absence of decay). Comparative anatomy: Homologous structures are anatomically similar structures in different species that have different functions, inherited from a common ancestor (e.g. the pentadactyl limb — human arm, whale flipper, bat wing, horse leg all share the same bone arrangement). This is evidence of divergent evolution. Analogous structures have similar functions but different origins (e.g. bird wing and insect wing) — evidence of convergent evolution. Vestigial structures are reduced, non-functional remnants of structures that were functional in ancestors (e.g. human appendix, tailbone, whale pelvic bones). Comparative biochemistry: DNA and protein sequence comparisons reveal evolutionary relationships — the more similar the sequences, the more closely related the species. All organisms share the same genetic code (universal code), supporting a common ancestor. Cytochrome c, a protein in the electron transport chain, is highly conserved across species — differences accumulate over time (molecular clock). Comparative embryology: Vertebrate embryos look very similar in early stages, with features like pharyngeal slits and tails — suggesting a shared evolutionary origin. Biogeography: The distribution of species across continents supports evolution — island species often resemble nearby mainland species but are distinct (e.g. Darwin's finches on the Galapagos Islands).",
