        content: "Genetic variation is the raw material for evolution. Sources of genetic variation include: (1) Mutation — a random, permanent change in the DNA base sequence. Gene mutations include point mutations (one base change — substitution, insertion, or deletion). Chromosome mutations include changes in chromosome number (e.g. Down syndrome — trisomy 21) or structure (deletion, inversion, translocation, duplication). Most mutations are neutral or harmful; rarely, a mutation is beneficial. Mutagens (UV light, X-rays, chemicals) increase the mutation rate. (2) Sexual reproduction introduces variation through independent assortment of chromosomes during meiosis (each gamete gets a random combination of maternal and paternal chromosomes — 2^23 = 8.4 million possible combinations in humans), crossing over (exchange of genetic material between homologous chromosomes during prophase I of meiosis, creating new allele combinations), and random fertilisation (any sperm can fuse with any egg). (3) Gene flow — movement of alleles between populations through migration. Genetic drift is a change in allele frequency due to random chance, most significant in small populations. The founder effect occurs when a small group colonises a new area, carrying only a subset of the original population's alleles. The bottleneck effect occurs when a population is drastically reduced (e.g. by a natural disaster), and the survivors' allele frequencies may not represent the original population. Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium describes a theoretical population where allele frequencies do not change — this requires no mutation, no selection, no migration, random mating, and a large population size. In reality, these conditions are never fully met, so populations evolve.",
